\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The figure displays exact coefficient matrices for $d=2$, $m=3$. The combined row support of two seeds has rank three. The bar heights record algebraic orbit dimensions and should not be read as probabilities of unitary state preparation.}}{1}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces This rational model uses two coordinates to illustrate block inversion. Both the eliminated block and the full operator are invertible, and the plotted values are exact fractions. The example shows why direct compression and resolvent elimination give different results.}}{2}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The figure shows exact weights for one supported code state. The normalized map preserves trace one, while the unnormalized positive map has output trace four. The illustration records this finite scaling distinction and does not assert a general recovery construction.}}{3}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The diagram shows the quantum realization of the common sequence of operators in the seed calculus. The same finite packet language describes states, gates, Hamiltonians, channels, geometric projectors, and effective dynamics.}}{4}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The eight computational packets of three qubits refine the two total-parity packets exactly.}}{8}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The Bell state generated from the seed $\left |00\right \rangle $.}}{10}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Circuit reconstruction as a sum over packet paths.}}{10}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The Hamiltonian becomes exactly block diagonal when the even and odd basis vectors are grouped.}}{11}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces A perturbation that breaks parity creates nonzero transfer between the packets. The measured leakage remains below the commutator bound.}}{12}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The equation in the moving frame holds to numerical precision only when the connection term is retained.}}{13}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The curvature of the Bloch projector, evaluated by finite differences, is compared with the exact density $\frac 12\sin \mittheta $.}}{16}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Amplitude damping transfers population from the excited packet to the ground packet while preserving trace and positivity.}}{18}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The four orthogonal syndrome packets of the three-qubit bit-flip code. Each packet identifies one correctable error without resolving the encoded logical state.}}{21}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Exact Feshbach reduction versus bare truncation.}}{22}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Norms of packet Green-function blocks.}}{22}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The diagram organizes a conjectural nested packet tower for quantum geometry. It does not provide a numerical reconstruction of physical spacetime.}}{24}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Positive spectrum of the Euclidean transfer operator.}}{25}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Ground-state volume packet in the controlled toy model.}}{25}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The figure shows the finite Euclidean kernel for volume transfer. Positivity and semigroup composition hold exactly in the finite model. Its gravitational interpretation remains provisional.}}{26}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces The first nonzero circle mode converges at second order and remains below the proved dispersion bound.}}{27}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces The curves show intermediate windows of spectral dimension for a finite cycle and a finite two-dimensional torus. Departures in the ultraviolet and infrared reflect the finite size of the models rather than additional dimensions.}}{28}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces The proof boundary. Finite packet, circuit, leakage, Feshbach, connection, and spectral identities are established here. Constraint closure and the physical continuum claims remain conjectural.}}{30}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces The finite mathematical layers developed in the article and the separate limit criteria needed at the continuum boundary.}}{35}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces The dimension reached by all local operators is linear in Schmidt rank.}}{37}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces An operator of charge one moves each cyclic packet to the next packet and has no other transfer blocks.}}{40}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces A circuit block is the coherent sum of its allowed packet paths. Selection rules remove forbidden paths before amplitudes are evaluated.}}{43}{figure.caption.96}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces Exact transition amplitude in a two packet model and the linear commutator bound.}}{44}{figure.caption.103}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces A moving rank one packet on the Bloch sphere. The Kato evolution transports the range without choosing a phase convention for its eigenvector.}}{49}{figure.caption.118}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces A density matrix or observable carries two packet addresses. Diagonal packets contain populations and internal observables. Off diagonal packets contain coherences.}}{51}{figure.caption.125}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Amplitude damping transfers population and contracts the coherence packet.}}{55}{figure.caption.140}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces The syndrome packets of the three qubit repetition code. Each packet has dimension two and carries the full logical state.}}{60}{figure.caption.155}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces The zeros of the Feshbach determinant recover the eigenvalues of the full finite Hamiltonian away from the hidden spectrum.}}{61}{figure.caption.160}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces A finite hidden spectrum produces an oscillatory memory kernel. The retained dynamics therefore depend on the history of the retained state.}}{64}{figure.caption.175}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces Eigenvalues of a finite reflection positive Gram matrix. Small numerical negative values would signal either roundoff or failure of the transfer assumptions.}}{67}{figure.caption.190}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces Finite periodic lattices show an intermediate dimension window. The ultraviolet and infrared limits return to zero because the state space is finite.}}{71}{figure.caption.207}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces The closure obstruction lives in a quotient by the constraint span. Physical compression is automatic for self adjoint constraints and cannot replace this quotient.}}{73}{figure.caption.212}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces A refinement tower carries Hamiltonians, packets, constraints, and transfer data. Each family has its own compatibility defect.}}{75}{figure.caption.219}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces The finite identities occupy the first step. Every later step needs a separate convergence or reconstruction theorem.}}{80}{figure.caption.234}%
